#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064045 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "69180c3b-7c26-5e5f-8d7a-42aed98431bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-3736" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of $f(x) = 2x - x^2$  on the interval $[-1,4].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2x-x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 2-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n2-2x = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=1.\n$$\n\nThe critical points are $x=1$ and the endpoints $x=-1,4$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)-(-1)^{2}=-3 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)-(1)^{2}=1 \\\\ f(4) & =2(4)-(4)^{2}=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=1,$ so the global maximum value is $1.$\n\n- The least value is $f(4)=-8,$ so the global minimum value is $-8.$\n\n![](images/c2/c277beb3423cc80d3ac2a001ac1063059b645c65fc8cb77978050f3831274b31.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064046 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064164 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "a0f47cd2-ea8f-5278-ab67-46ecd65d2f4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3737" :question/problem "Find the global maximum and minimum values for the function $f(x) = x^4 - 6 x^2$ on the closed interval $[-1, 3].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x^4 - 6x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 4x^3 - 12x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-12x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-3) & =0 \\\\ 4x(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x = -\\sqrt 3 \\approx -1.73$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,3]$, we ignore this point.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,\\sqrt{3}$ and the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at the critical points:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(-1) \t& = (-1)^4 - 6(-1)^2 = -5\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(0) \t\t& = (0)^4 - 6(0)^2 = 0\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(\\sqrt 3) \t& = (\\sqrt 3)^4 - 6(\\sqrt 3)^2 = -9\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(3) \t\t& = (3)^4 - 6(3)^2 = 27 \\\t\t\n \t\t}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the global maximum is $27$ and the global minimum is $-9.$\n\n![](images/74/74ce5114dbc93035b0be078be12149cced66ce881dfbc37768ed9febcdfcba87.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ac06203-f450-56e6-bf00-f392cec8e021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446053} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf8af1a-e65d-55f1-b5aa-0f6499f2e0f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446058} {:answer/id #uuid "9ddd95b3-37c0-5ea8-91de-762536f3b73e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446059} {:answer/id #uuid "a6844010-1c33-526d-ac0d-05736327a1ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446062} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc3be7e-6fd6-5b52-bdfd-43707a36bdc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcc3be7e-6fd6-5b52-bdfd-43707a36bdc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446066} :answer-field/id #uuid "b81fbc27-5dd0-5c38-b035-b8bc202627e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5c74e5b-a85a-561f-a53c-37938e4b9091" :question/math-academy-id "q-113771" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}+1){e}^{x},$ defined on the closed interval $[-2,0],$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The global minimum value is $\\frac{5}{{e}^{2}}$\n\nII. The global minimum value is $\\frac{2}{e}$\n\nIII. The global maximum value is $1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=({x}^{2}+1){e}^{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x{e}^{x}+({x}^{2}+1){e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{x} \\\\  & =(x+1)^{2}{e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}{e}^{x}=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=-1,$ and the endpoints $x=-2,0$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =((-2)^{2}+1){e}^{-2}=\\frac{5}{{e}^{2}}≈0.68 \\\\ f(-1) & =((-1)^{2}+1){e}^{-1}=\\frac{2}{e}≈0.74 \\\\ f(0) & =({0}^{2}+1){e}^{0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(0)=1,$ so the global maximum is $1.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(-2)=\\frac{5}{{e}^{2}},$ so the global minimum is $\\frac{5}{{e}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"\n\n![](images/87/8768b85a24b4351a481fec2801be274990b2e524a7322bfcf07b257884b9409f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b745eac-288c-519f-827e-2937ae90553b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061615} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a41a38-3f10-5342-9dc5-04a2ecfcc001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061616} {:answer/id #uuid "3df2c088-75da-58f8-942c-5d4b3a14d502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061617} {:answer/id #uuid "419036f6-f9dd-5b25-a7a0-84c4a9c291ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061618} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6555da-0526-5464-be80-e4fe3bd7facd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3df2c088-75da-58f8-942c-5d4b3a14d502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061617} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d6c3185-c6b2-5724-9230-4a66915228ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2435a5d9-0223-53db-acdd-e3c8030d82a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-20948" :question/problem "Find  the global maximum value of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ on the closed interval $\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le 2.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all real values of $x.$ So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ \n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\frac{1}{2})=2,$ so the global maximum is $2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global minimum is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n![](images/ae/aef1d6e33213def71ada4a59f7d79b9f045083b2c501a752e81994e33f100277.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064164 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45594262-3efe-57c7-b5b9-bfdbc6564c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290917} {:answer/id #uuid "7620126f-9b05-57e1-83c0-abc96aed6552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291136} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb07632-b195-5870-b2a7-66c1d64cb5d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291169} {:answer/id #uuid "a844a83e-0aac-539f-89a3-bca1ef965168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291368} {:answer/id #uuid "fba97015-0947-55e9-a31d-e68b17721999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eb07632-b195-5870-b2a7-66c1d64cb5d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291169} :answer-field/id #uuid "966e70db-9154-5f74-a132-3c4d2bdeac39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bea0a781-9898-5e25-a447-5b87446089a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-2690" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+9x+3,$ on the closed interval $-4\\le x\\le -2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The global maximum value is $3$\n\nII. The global minimum value is $1$\n\nIII. The global minimum value is $-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+9x+3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+12x+9 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+4x+3) \\\\  & =3(x+1)(x+3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n3(x+1)(x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,\\,-1.\n$$\n\nSince $x=-1$ does not belong to the interval $[-4,-2]$, the critical points are $x=-3$ and the endpoints $x=-4,-2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =(-4)^{3}+6(-4)^{2}+9(-4)+3=-1 \\\\ f(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+6(-3)^{2}+9(-3)+3=3 \\\\ f(-2) & =(-2)^{3}+6(-2)^{2}+9(-2)+3=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-3)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The least value is $f(-4)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"\n\n![](images/fa/fa85119166ca325f9925ddd03a578fb5daae463c59c3b7b9f7ac0f6464a30241.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064164 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a8313dd-5fdd-5c81-a1b5-48c8c4a34af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290805} {:answer/id #uuid "33ed80ac-0b34-5d04-b4be-e2a0347c210b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290842} {:answer/id #uuid "4a244d28-9365-55e7-81e4-fdf02c2a2c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290940} {:answer/id #uuid "6c95327d-f3a6-5aaa-8c47-7b4e3d402e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291099} {:answer/id #uuid "76c4c7f4-f5c8-5499-94aa-d57c71cb7050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76c4c7f4-f5c8-5499-94aa-d57c71cb7050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291138} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1d95fed-6abb-5898-9ccd-fc77f7d79a43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8383b31-3ef6-56e1-8528-1bcdbd1b3b84" :question/math-academy-id "q-2721" :question/problem "Find  the global minimum value of the function $f(x)=3{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+3$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=3{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 12{x}^{2}(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,1]$, the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =3(-1)^{4}-8(-1)^{3}+3=14 \\\\ f(0) & =3(0)^{4}-8(0)^{3}+3=3 \\\\ f(1) & =3(1)^{4}-8(1)^{3}+3=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=14,$ so the global maximum is $14.$\n\n- The least value is $f(1)=-2,$ so the global minimum is $-2.$\n\n![](images/9a/9ac7f0c0e28532c897e1c89a35e9c027f614fee40b4b94362a6c5cb012c8a7f6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446079 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064164 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14e95874-53a9-59eb-a59d-1506787a86d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446051} {:answer/id #uuid "28e244bd-f5d9-59e7-bae1-37bf7dad0604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446052} {:answer/id #uuid "83b3d4b4-9193-548b-92d2-89f90a26ba3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446057} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0fca65-f9f6-5b4c-986e-f335607b1999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446063} {:answer/id #uuid "cca7c6b4-7bda-5ecf-9402-b6c9fb9f1393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cca7c6b4-7bda-5ecf-9402-b6c9fb9f1393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446065} :answer-field/id #uuid "eafc929c-fdf4-5237-8e6e-4e3e669ade96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d6fccce-a1a6-5495-8484-833c6aab421e" :question/math-academy-id "q-3011" :question/problem "Find the global maximum value of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le 2\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{4},$ since they are the only values that belong to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4},$ and the endpoints $x=0,\\,2\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=-1 \\\\ f(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ f(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ f(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\sqrt{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=-\\sqrt{2},$ so the global minimum is $-\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n![](images/76/7645cecb4fef2af4f2e2061752957981414d63700de016ca4a535c8df219597e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52810d71-5114-5feb-ae18-a48ef7e104ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446054} {:answer/id #uuid "5e847e93-be00-5b15-bfac-543002b0dde8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446055} {:answer/id #uuid "8141d386-0404-52e8-8de7-454bc213ddad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446056} {:answer/id #uuid "a1847c55-c1ef-5d11-9f47-66ad60e3ea46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446060} {:answer/id #uuid "a675690c-021e-5075-9cda-7de9a1641938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e847e93-be00-5b15-bfac-543002b0dde8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446055} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecc7805a-aaf8-5454-ad50-b7b1a50aac12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4194a834-6555-5df2-9764-fffba77d3eaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-3012" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3),$ defined on the closed interval $-2\\le x\\le 0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The global maximum value is $1$\n\nII. The global maximum value is $0$\n\nIII. The global minimum value is $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{x+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all values of $x\\in [-2,0].$  So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=-2,0$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-2+3)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)=0 \\\\ f(0) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+3)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)≈-1.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-2)=0,$ so the global maximum is $0.$\n\n- The least value is $f(0)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3),$ so the global minimum is $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\n![](images/85/8578b190927c38e37fc5956d1b953539e41ea3e25f2e6ced48f21086722fdd43.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9151074f-2d08-535c-a8e8-72d9cbef933e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9151074f-2d08-535c-a8e8-72d9cbef933e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315928} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1a59685-a019-5aa4-aeb6-c0eab15154c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50b35122-a792-5902-a8d9-65288095b044" :question/math-academy-id "q-344373" :question/problem "The global maximum value of the function $f(x)=-x+4$ on the closed interval $1\\le x\\le 5$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-x+4,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all real values of $x.$ So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=1,5$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-(1)+4=3 \\\\ f(5) & =-(5)+4=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(5)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](images/b6/b66a275ad0f733f9b7a715cacc87e47ab42200def67a3a7f5a5e577ba067a1c3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0070102-4be6-521a-a2c3-b7b69545cf7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0070102-4be6-521a-a2c3-b7b69545cf7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446067} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dd21141-a64a-59a6-830b-705be2c9af59" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f007c55-3624-5699-862a-6b2181c2b793" :question/math-academy-id "q-344386" :question/problem "The global minimum value of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{x+5}$ on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le 4$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{x}{x+5},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{5}{(x+5)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all real values of $x.$ So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=0,4$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{0}{(0+5)}=0 \\\\ f(4) & =\\frac{4}{(4+5)}=\\frac{4}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(4)=\\frac{4}{9},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{4}{9}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(0)=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\n![](images/73/731aff75eb5930d89e1306c44c34986ba2f92b5761971dcc9cbe3571963f2ab2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064164 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c57ecb3d-6b4a-5770-a83e-208ec453a7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c57ecb3d-6b4a-5770-a83e-208ec453a7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446064} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d8e368b-ffbe-5e66-824e-4093e6579f11" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4034162d-ab25-585a-b5bc-dfd3804a541e" :question/math-academy-id "q-344434" :question/problem "The exact global minimum value of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+2$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-6x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x & =0 \\\\ 3x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,1],$ the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1)^{3}-3(-1)^{2}+2=-1-3+2=-2 \\\\ f(0) & =(0)^{3}-3(0)^{2}+2=2 \\\\ f(1) & =(1)^{3}-3(1)^{2}+2=1-3+2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(0)=2,$ so the global maximum is $2.$\n\n- The least value is $f(-1)=-2,$ so the global minimum is $-2.$\n\n![](images/39/3939bd8d9b8efd0be9381087b9b1802ee9bebd4fad620b855904f87d3bc61a86.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446075 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064164 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f860dfb5-9cc2-564b-a719-e277d51c0fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f860dfb5-9cc2-564b-a719-e277d51c0fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446068} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d0bc8af-0212-5f26-b710-65114290ccbb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "264068ae-fe2b-5f78-b146-741b451e1198" :question/math-academy-id "q-344474" :question/problem "The exact global maximum value of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le 2\\pi $ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$ since they are the only values that belong to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$ and the endpoints $x=0,\\,2\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1 \\\\ f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ f\\,(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & =sin\\,(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+cos\\,(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}=-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ f(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\sqrt{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- The least value is $f\\,(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=-\\sqrt{2},$ so the global minimum is $-\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n![](images/e4/e42ca640ae08d60ea81bdcb7c3c3753f5dc74fb6eea49b5b0f999e3463b5f8f0.png)"}]}
